title The weave merges, and the module nobody compiled
prompt The baton, plus the seat's own lane: mantra and tally. The seat prompt names the next real work as the MERGE of two weaves and the annotation that shows what each side did, correcting REDS %441's re-cut arc.
think Opened with the cold roster pass and held still while it ran -- 115 guards, 108 green, 3 red, 4 gated, 852s, tree_moved=no. Two reds are the ones the card already owns; remember_git_nib is a third and new on this tree.
think Rota: git rev-list --count HEAD reads 3990, so 3990 mod 5 = 0, the aether row -- which the previous lap already read at N=3985. Advanced by hand to row 1, Air/Saturn, law and boundary: the threshold page, TAME_CORE, single-stranded, Gauge. The right row for a lap about to write Rye, and its sense is touch: press each fence and see whether the hand goes through.
think Reds first. %431 sits at custody gate %1 and %360's remainder is explicitly in other lanes, so both surface rather than route around. %440 is BOOKED with its remainder a booked lap in the session-logs lane. My own lane's queue was not empty, so claim-as-override did not apply.
think Pressed the module's fences before adding to it. mantra/src/weave.rye carries seven asserts, three allocating calls, and ZERO named maxima -- TAME root rule 1 is bound everything, and this module has 120 files reaching toward it. Measured the four TAME meters: tame_style_check has no mention of bound, max, or ceiling; width-check reads widths; rune_assert_sweep reads assert presence; invariant_coverage reads assert LABELS. Not one reads whether an allocation names a max.
think Designed merge as a join before writing it, so the properties come from the algebra rather than from tests. Union of positions, max on gen, max on next_pos -- each commutative, associative, idempotent, so merge is too by construction. Sorting the union by position turns document-equality into field-for-field equality.
think Refusal is order-independent too, and I wrote down why: weaves that pairwise agree on shared positions are closed under merge, since the merged text at a position comes from one of the two sides and both already agreed with the third. So no grouping can hide a disagreement from a later one.
think The build refused on the first try, and the refusal was the lap's real find. Weave.empty() returns .{ .lines = .{} }, the elder ArrayList form; Zig 0.16 wants .empty. Copied the HEAD blob into a pen and built it alone: missing struct field: items. The module has not compiled since the toolchain moved.
think Checked who builds it before claiming nothing does. grep -rn 'rye build' tools/ names mantra/src/main.rye at three sites and mantra/src/weave.rye at zero. main.rye carries its own inlined Weave; rye/tests/mantra_weave_test.rye inlines it again and reads .empty at three sites -- the copy migrated because the copy is compiled.
think Wrote the control before believing the witness. Seven phases in a pen: clean, join, order, text, bound_shrunk, bound_removed, bound_misnamed. The bound phases shrink max_weave_lines to 8 first, because deleting the check at 1<<20 would run for hours rather than fail.
obs MY FIRST SEVEN CLAIMS COULD NOT REACH THE SORT. Deleting std.mem.sort from merge left claims 1 through 7 all printing GREEN -- measured, not reasoned -- because every union in them is already position-ordered. Claim 8 plants an interleaving union and the run stops there, at merge's own strictly-increasing postcondition. A control leg that cannot fail is decorative, and this one was until claim 8 existed.
obs GREP PROVES A FILE'S SHAPE; ONLY A COMPILER PROVES A FILE. Four rostered tier lap guards read weave.rye by grep -q -- the field names of Line, Weave, and Diff, and the string gen >= 1 -- and all four were GREEN in this lap's cold pass, over a file the compiler refuses. They hold the module's shape perfectly while it has stopped being a program.
obs THE IDENTITY GAP IS REAL AND I NAMED IT RATHER THAN PAPERING OVER IT. Two branches that both insert are handed the same position by their own counters, so merge refuses them PositionTextDisagrees rather than keeping one line and dropping the other. pos names a line inside one weave and cannot name one across two. Closing it wants a wider Line, and mantra_glow_tend_limb1 locks Line to three fields against a Glow shape -- a seam, so it waits for Keaton's word.
obs A LOG-ONLY FOLLOW-UP IS A NIB-STALING COMMIT. The previous lap pinned the card's nib correctly at its own parent, then committed 9c8cd4acb to record what the send actually took. Those facts do not exist until the send is over, and by then the commit that would carry them is pushed -- so the follow-up is structural rather than sloppy, and remember_git_nib red on this lap's open.
obs THE LONG-FUNCTION RATCHET CANNOT SEE A FUNCTION HOLDING AN INLINE COMPARATOR. tame_style_long_fn_one.sh restarts its awk on any line matching indented `fn `, and a std.mem.sort comparator is exactly that. merge stood at 72 lines and read clean. Split at union_into anyway; the meter's blindness is a sibling find rather than an excuse.
obs THE CARD WAS 453 BYTES UNDER ITS BOUND WITH TWO REDS TO BOOK. Marked a checkpoint cairn first, then folded four landed accounts to a dated shelf -- %438, %430, %441, %439, and Petrichor's mark-law weave -- each replaced by a pointer, each also standing in its own REDS row.
obs THE SPINE COLLIDED FOUR TIMES IN ONE SEND AND THE LAW DECIDED EVERY ONE WITHOUT JUDGMENT. Booked %445 and %446 from a clean --next read against a fetched xy; a peer published its own %445 (stamp 20260905.224445) mid-lap, so mine shifted to %446 and %447; a second peer published %446 (stamp 20260905.230021), a third published %447 (stamp 20260905.233110), and a fourth published %448 (stamp 20260906.002530), so across four re-integrations mine wore five numbers and landed at %449 and %450. Theirs are published and frozen, both of mine were unshared every time, and no judgment entered any of the four moves.
obs MY FOLD LOST TO A PUBLISHED FOLD, WHICH IS THE SAME LAW ONE LEVEL UP. I folded rows %439 and %441 to my own shelf to hold the pin under bound; a peer folded %439 through %441 to REDS-what-no-meter-was-reading in the same hour. Two shelves holding one row would have read as a duplicate to the monotone scan, so my shelf and its recital line were dropped whole and theirs kept -- a fold is a published fact the moment it lands upstream, exactly as a row number is.
obs THE PEER'S CARD MISNAMED A ROW AND THE LEDGER SETTLED IT. Upstream's live front wrote %440 CLOSED over the comlink topology account, where the ledger binds stamp 20260905.224714 to %439 -- checked against xy/main before resolving rather than reasoned about. The conflict took my side for that line, and the peer's row itself is untouched.
obs THE REBASE CLOSED A RED UNDER ME. Upstream's publish-seed.sh reconstruction landed mid-lap, so commit_message_guard reads GREEN on this tree now and %431 is closed here; remember_git_nib is green with it. Post-rebase re-verify: 117 guards, 112 green, 1 red, 4 gated, 774s, and the one red is agent_jail's environment fact. The receipt stays withheld, since one red withholds it whatever it is.
obs THIS LAP FOLLOWS THE RULE ITS OWN ROW PROPOSES. Correcting the card's pass reading needed a second commit, which is exactly %447's shape -- so that commit carries the card's Git nib forward to its own parent rather than leaving the card describing the round before it.
obs THE WORK COMMIT'S BODY FROZE THE ELDER NUMBERS, AND THE PEER HAD ALREADY SEATED THE ANSWER. It names %446 and %447 where the rows now read %449 and %450. A commit body is testimony and keeps every word it wrote, so each row carries an erratum naming its own stamp as the identity that held through every number -- the practice upstream's own `the number a commit body froze at` seated in the same hour, one row over.
